// QUARANTINED: not implemented, not loadable, on purpose.
// Was std/core/option.orb. See std/quarantine/README.md for why it is
// not a .orb file.
//
// MISSING LANGUAGE FEATURE: a generic tagged union. Two things are
// missing, and the first one hides the second.
//
//   1. The parser has no generic parameter list on `union`.
//      parseTypeDecl calls parseGenericParams for `fn`, `model` and
//      `trait` (compiler/parser.orb:690, 769, 946) but not on the
//      `union` and `enum` arms (compiler/parser.orb:812-850), so
//      `union Option<T>` stops at the `<`.
//         $ printf 'import "std/core/option.orb"\nfn main() { print("ok") }\n' > /tmp/i.orb
//         $ orbit check /tmp/i.orb
//         Import error: std/core/option.orb:5: Expected '{' after 'union'.
//         Import error: std/core/option.orb:5: Expected variant name.
//
//   2. Even with [T] instead of <T>, there is no monomorphisation, so
//      a type parameter would be nonsense rather than merely
//      unparseable. The payload type is a bare identifier
//      (compiler/parser.orb:840), so `T` would reach codegen as a
//      type name and be emitted verbatim:
//         model Box[T] { v: T }
//         fn main() { val b: Box = Box { v: 7 }  print(b.v) }
//         orbit check  -> no errors
//         orbit run    -> <build>:74:5: error: unknown type name 'T'
//                          74 |     T* v;
//      `orbit check` accepts a generic model and `orbit build` then
//      hands the C compiler a `T` that does not exist. That is a
//      compiler bug in its own right (filed as F-0007); it also means
//      the honest spelling for a payload-carrying union today is a
//      concrete payload type per variant, e.g.
//         union OptInt { SomeInt(int)  NoInt }
//
// This file is kept as the specification of the wanted feature, NOT
// as a working module. It is not imported by anything and it is not
// covered by tests/std/. Deleting it would lose the only written
// record that Orbit wants Option; shipping it as-is would be a file
// that claims a type the language cannot express.
//
// Note the asymmetry with `result`: that one already exists, as a
// builtin type with `ok`/`err` constructors (compiler/sema.orb:236,
// compiler/lexer.orb:199-200). std/core/result.orb was an unparseable
// shadow of a working builtin and was deleted; this one is the only
// trace of a feature that does not exist yet.
//
// LIFT THE QUARANTINE WHEN: the parser accepts a type parameter list
// on `union` AND codegen substitutes the argument at each use
// (`Option<int>` yielding distinct C for `Option<string>`). Then
// rename this back to std/core/option.orb, give it a test under
// tests/std/, and ask the docs agent for a LANGUAGE_REFERENCE entry.
union Option<T> {
    Some(T),
    None
}
